WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.310
Speaker 1: So I want to start today talking
about inflation, we are now starting to see

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:10.380
inflation rise more quickly than it has in
quite some time, and Republicans are going

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:17.320
absolutely crazy, thinking that this vindicates
their 30 years of claims that, oh, no, now

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.250
the government has done some stimulus.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:25.360
We are going to see this inflationary spiral
which will destroy the economy.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.199
They've been saying it for 30 years and it
hasn't happened.

00:00:28.199 --> 00:00:35.780
And now they are turning this recent new employment
inflation number, not employment number, into

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:40.420
what they claim is a vindication of the claims
they've been making for the last 30 years.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:43.680
And somehow this also relates to Joe Biden.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.090
And we're going to get back to that in a moment.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:51.110
But I think it's important to understand the
kind of context here the U.S. has been.

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:57.270
I guess you could say the word is blessed
with very low inflation for a long time, steady

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:03.780
but low inflation, the way most economists
believe is ideal for an economy.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.270
And we'll talk about why a little bit later.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:12.609
The consumer price index has now increased
in April compared to a year earlier by four

00:01:12.609 --> 00:01:14.009
point two percent.

00:01:14.009 --> 00:01:20.100
And this is the most in a year over year comparison
dating all the way back to September of 2008.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:26.889
So in about 13 years, when we compare one
month in one year to that same month, the

00:01:26.889 --> 00:01:31.719
year before, this is the biggest change we've
seen about in the last 13 years.

00:01:31.719 --> 00:01:36.740
Economists surveyed by Dow Jones were looking
for an increase of three point six percent.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.310
It came in above that at four point two percent.

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:42.959
Now, this is really, really important to understand.

00:01:42.959 --> 00:01:49.469
Before we even get into the macroeconomic
analysis, I do think we are seeing some prices

00:01:49.469 --> 00:01:51.869
truly increase in some areas.

00:01:51.869 --> 00:01:58.609
Lumber, for example, as far as the research
I've done shows lumber is getting more expensive

00:01:58.609 --> 00:02:05.939
in a real way, not just because of a pandemic
related statistical artifact, but much of

00:02:05.939 --> 00:02:11.950
this increase is merely a pandemic related
statistical artifact.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:19.430
Gas prices, for example, going up in late
2020 and so far in 2021, aside from this temporary

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:24.120
pipeline shutdown, which is being resolved,
is back on.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:30.819
As of today, we saw one of the biggest demand
collapses for gasoline in 2020 because the

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:34.019
country shut down, people weren't driving,
people weren't commuting.

00:02:34.019 --> 00:02:39.260
And then relative to that, we saw a huge demand
increase later in 2020 and particularly in

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:40.610
2021.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:47.730
So the numbers we just received for inflation
are April of twenty twenty one compared to

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.299
April of twenty twenty.

00:02:49.299 --> 00:02:55.130
In April of twenty twenty, we had one of the
biggest macroeconomic demand collapses in

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:56.190
a very long time.

00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:00.800
I can't I don't remember when we had such
a demand collapse because it was the first

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.269
full month of covid shutdown.

00:03:03.269 --> 00:03:05.420
So prices collapsed.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:09.299
Now we are comparing to April of twenty twenty.

00:03:09.299 --> 00:03:15.760
And so when you look at the internals of what's
driving this price increase, its energy prices,

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:21.510
again, hugely demand driven oil prices collapsed
in the second quarter of twenty twenty, where

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:24.780
at one point oil futures had even gone negative.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.409
Now people want to travel again.

00:03:26.409 --> 00:03:27.409
People want to drive.

00:03:27.409 --> 00:03:28.690
People want to fly.

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:34.329
Used car and truck prices are up, which makes
sense as some people are going back to commuting

00:03:34.329 --> 00:03:35.329
one car.

00:03:35.329 --> 00:03:36.599
Families are going back to two cars.

00:03:36.599 --> 00:03:42.110
All of the people who got there first who
would have gotten their first jobs and potentially

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:47.569
gotten a car during the pandemic, but were
either working from home or didn't even get

00:03:47.569 --> 00:03:49.069
that first job until now.

00:03:49.069 --> 00:03:52.360
Many of them now need vehicles to start getting
to and from work.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:58.849
So this is primarily what we call a base rate
effect when we compare inflation last month

00:03:58.849 --> 00:04:04.859
to inflation at a time when lots of prices
had collapsed, you are going to see a relative

00:04:04.859 --> 00:04:10.569
price increase that may not really be representative
of what is going on in the economy.

00:04:10.569 --> 00:04:15.139
Now, the good news is that the Fed seems to
know this and has said we're not going to

00:04:15.139 --> 00:04:18.310
do anything in the immediate as a result of
these inflation numbers.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:23.169
One month of numbers with the heart of the
pandemic as the base number that we're comparing

00:04:23.169 --> 00:04:26.620
to April of twenty twenty, it really doesn't
give you much to work with.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:30.720
The inflation target remains two percent for
the year.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:37.150
The Fed still expects that yearly inflation
year over year will be roughly two percent.

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:39.250
So for now, the Fed isn't going to raise rates.

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:40.720
They're not going to make any other changes.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:43.440
Now, that all being said.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:49.330
There are actual shortages of some construction
materials that will probably remain for the

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:51.660
foreseeable future, and that's going to cost
more.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.370
Renovations are going to cost more.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:55.400
New home prices may well cost more.

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.620
Now, let me address a couple of other bigger
picture things.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:04.060
First, you might be saying, David, sir, why
is the target two percent inflation like?

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:05.860
Why is some inflation better than none?

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:08.900
Shouldn't we be targeting zero inflation?

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:11.460
This is a very debated economic issue.

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:17.770
But fundamentally, if you have deflation or
you have stagnant prices, meaning no change

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:22.039
whatsoever, it's an incentive for people to
wait to purchase things.

00:05:22.039 --> 00:05:24.500
Now, this is a standard economic analysis.

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:29.259
If you want to apply a totally different line
of thinking to the economy, a more socialistic

00:05:29.259 --> 00:05:33.250
line of thinking communistic, of course, you're
going to have a different perspective.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:38.220
But in the kind of traditional economic thinking,
which is, of course, only partially right,

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:43.000
we know now that classical economics is only
tells part of the story.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:48.210
The idea is, if you think things will cost
more in the future, you are incentivized to

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:50.380
buy them now before the prices go up.

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:53.010
You get it for less now and you can start
using it now.

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:54.060
Why would you wait?

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:58.780
That is where growth and employment and wages
come from.

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:02.220
If you think things will be cheaper in the
future, or at least that they won't be more

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:08.770
expensive, you might wait to see if prices
go down and then it creates a deflationary

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:12.020
spiral because you're waiting to buy.

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:13.250
Prices do go down.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:17.960
Businesses have to lay people off since they're
not selling stuff because remember, you're

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.259
waiting to buy.

00:06:19.259 --> 00:06:23.360
They don't have the employees that get laid
off, don't have money to buy stuff.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:27.690
So prices go down more and you can have what's
called a deflationary spiral.

00:06:27.690 --> 00:06:31.479
Japan experienced this for a long period of
time.

00:06:31.479 --> 00:06:36.449
It's neoclassical at basic economic thinking,
but that's part of the working theory.

00:06:36.449 --> 00:06:41.440
Obviously, too much inflation is bad because
then people's purchasing power goes down.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:42.780
They can't afford stuff either.

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:47.750
So that's where you get to this kind of happy
medium of we want a little bit of inflation

00:06:47.750 --> 00:06:48.790
and predictably so.

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:53.030
Now, last thing on this, Republicans are now
claiming vindication.

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:59.150
We told you the stimulus would make prices
go up and things would get unaffordable.

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:02.919
They said this one, Biden did the one point
nine trillion dollar stimulus.

00:07:02.919 --> 00:07:07.680
They didn't say this when Trump did both of
his stimulus packages.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:12.500
They have been saying it for thirty years,
including in 2009 when Obama did TARP, et

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:13.500
cetera, et cetera.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:14.500
The truth is inflation.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:19.169
Throughout all of that, government spending
has been very well controlled and very modest

00:07:19.169 --> 00:07:20.870
and very reasonable.

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:25.980
This seems to be primarily an artifact of
comparing numbers to the absolute lul, the

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:28.960
doldrums of the pandemic.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:30.030
And that's it.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:34.409
There's really I'm finding myself not even
really having more to say about that piece

00:07:34.409 --> 00:07:36.460
because it is so transparently untrue.

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:38.349
So that's the latest on inflation.

00:07:38.349 --> 00:07:39.349
We'll watch the numbers.

00:07:39.349 --> 00:07:41.069
We will soon have numbers in early June.

00:07:41.069 --> 00:07:45.740
We'll have numbers comparing May twenty, twenty
one to May, twenty twenty.

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:50.069
And then we'll have to at that point, we'll
be able to get a more well rounded picture

00:07:50.069 --> 00:08:51.280
of what's 

00:08:51.280 --> 00:09:25.370
going on.

